1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
And now, to you, Scoutmaster.

2
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:13,000
So, uh, I was down to camp the other day, went in the trading post, the aquatic director was there.

3
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:18,000
I got... I'm a little worried about him. He was trying to cash a buddy check.

4
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:24,000
Think about it. It'll come to you. Hang on. There it is.

5
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:29,000
Hey, this is podcast number 71.

6
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:32,000
Hey!

7
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:34,000
Hey!

8
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:36,000
Hey!

9
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:02,000
Well, welcome back to the Scoutmaster podcast. This is Clark Green.

10
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:05,000
Let's check in and see who got in touch this week.

11
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:07,000
Mike Duff wrote in and said,

12
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:09,000
I discovered your podcast last summer.

13
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:13,000
And as a new Scoutmaster, it's been a tremendous resource.

14
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:16,000
There have been many aha moments listening to your podcast.

15
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:20,000
Yeah, I'm like, there's a couple of aha moments every now and again,

16
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:23,000
but there's some real ooh moments, too.

17
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:27,000
Thank you for all the time it must take to put it together.

18
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:31,000
And please know that it's been making a big impact on the Scouts and families

19
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:35,000
of Troop 381 in Prairie Village, Kansas.

20
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:38,000
Well, thanks very much, Mike, for your kind words.

21
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:41,000
Hello, Troop 381. Hello, Prairie Village, Kansas.

22
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:43,000
Good to hear from you all.

23
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:45,000
Alan Green wrote in and said this,

24
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:48,000
I had three sons in two troops.

25
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:50,000
They're all grown up now.

26
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:52,000
I have to admit that with my first son,

27
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:55,000
I probably stepped in more than I should have.

28
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:58,000
I was an assistant Scoutmaster, one among several,

29
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:00,000
and the Scoutmaster finally had a pal well

30
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:02,000
with all of us dads who were involved

31
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:04,000
and said that we got a little too emotional

32
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:06,000
when it came to our own sons.

33
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:08,000
He suggested, just like you did,

34
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:10,000
that it might be a good idea

35
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:12,000
if we worked with somebody else's son

36
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:14,000
and let our sons, you know, kind of do scouting.

37
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:16,000
I adopted the advice

38
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:20,000
and the second son was left more to himself than the first.

39
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:23,000
The third son joined another troop and I followed.

40
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:25,000
I think the best time for my sons was

41
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:27,000
when I was watching from a distance.

42
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:29,000
On two occasions,

43
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:31,000
I didn't make summer camp with the troop

44
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:33,000
and my boys struggled a bit,

45
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:35,000
but they overcame it without me

46
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:37,000
and I think they were the better for it.

47
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:40,000
I see parents now make the same mistakes I did.

48
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:42,000
They hover over their boy

49
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:44,000
and they don't realize that scouting

50
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:46,000
is a time for a boy to be away from home

51
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:48,000
and discover the world and society

52
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:50,000
apart from his family.

53
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:52,000
I would like to tell them

54
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:54,000
to work a little bit

55
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:56,000
and let scouting, the scout master

56
00:02:57,000 --> 00:02:58,000
and the troop leaders do their work.

57
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:01,000
Well, I think that's great advice, Alan,

58
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:03,000
and I really appreciate you being in touch.

59
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:05,000
Let them live their own lives a little bit.

60
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:07,000
It's really difficult

61
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:09,000
when you're a parent and a scout leader

62
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:11,000
at the same time.

63
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:13,000
It's hard to find the right perspective,

64
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:15,000
the right amount of involvement.

65
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:17,000
You know, it's a puzzle for all of us,

66
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:20,000
but there's Alan encouraging everybody

67
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:22,000
that maybe a little bit less

68
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:24,000
is more for most of the scouts.

69
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:27,000
Andre Crawford wrote in on Facebook.

70
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:29,000
You can find us on Facebook

71
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:31,000
at Scout Master Blog.

72
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:33,000
Go on Facebook, search for Scout Master Blog

73
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:35,000
and hey, there we are.

74
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:37,000
And Andre joined us there

75
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:39,000
and said I was finally able

76
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:41,000
to listen to the podcast last night.

77
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:43,000
Thank you so much for taking the time

78
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:45,000
to discuss these questions.

79
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:47,000
I feel it can only be a benefit

80
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:49,000
to those of us who are new to this.

81
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:51,000
Well, thank you, Andre,

82
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:52,000
I hope to have you aboard.

83
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:54,000
Andre's asked a couple of questions

84
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:56,000
about being a scout parent

85
00:03:57,000 --> 00:03:58,000
and working along with his son.

86
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:00,000
I think we were able to help out

87
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:02,000
a little bit there, Andre.

88
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:04,000
A lot of folks online left some comments

89
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:06,000
and some encouragement.

90
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:08,000
Speaking about online,

91
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:10,000
over at the Scout Master Blog,

92
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:12,000
this week we have a caption contest.

93
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:14,000
You can find the Scout Master Blog

94
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:16,000
by going to scoutmastercg.com.

95
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:19,000
And take a look at the caption contest.

96
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:20,000
It was pretty popular this week.

97
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:22,000
A lot of people signed on to look at it

98
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:24,000
and offer a caption

99
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:26,000
for a kind of humorous photo

100
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:28,000
that one of the readers sent in.

101
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:30,000
And I would love to have you come

102
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:32,000
and take a look at it

103
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:34,000
and vote for your favorite caption.

104
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:36,000
If the photos keep coming,

105
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:38,000
maybe we can make it a weekly occurrence.

106
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:40,000
So go on to scoutmastercg.com

107
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:42,000
and take a look.

108
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:44,000
And there's a wonderful,

109
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:46,000
impressive coveted prize

110
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:48,000
for the winning caption.

111
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:50,000
It's a May giveaway for the May

112
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:52,000
Scout Master newsletter.

113
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:54,000
And that prize,

114
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:56,000
which is a right in the rain

115
00:04:57,000 --> 00:04:58,000
outdoor journal kit,

116
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:00,000
goes to Phil Peck,

117
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:02,000
Scout Master of Troop 141

118
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:04,000
and Kokolala, Indiana.

119
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:06,000
So Phil, congratulations.

120
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:08,000
Thanks for answering the question

121
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:10,000
that qualified you

122
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:12,000
to be randomly selected

123
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:14,000
from all of those who gave

124
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:16,000
the right answer for that month's giveaway.

125
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:18,000
Finally here, let's see,

126
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:20,000
and Chris said this,

127
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:22,000
Clark, thanks for the story

128
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:24,000
of a scout dad.

129
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:26,000
I was listening to your podcast yesterday

130
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:28,000
and I thought it was great.

131
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:30,000
I'm the father of two scouts.

132
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:32,000
One's an Eagle Scout

133
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:34,000
and one's a new Tenderfoot.

134
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:36,000
At a troop court of honor last night,

135
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:38,000
one of my sons received his Silver Palm

136
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:40,000
and one of them received his Tenderfoot rank.

137
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:42,000
I reflected on how short a time

138
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:44,000
all of this is

139
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:46,000
and how blessed I am to be a part of it.

140
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:48,000
Thank you for your wonderful podcast.

141
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:50,000
I select an adult volunteer each year.

142
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:52,000
They think makes the most difference

143
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:54,000
in their lives.

144
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:56,000
At the conclusion of the court of honor,

145
00:05:57,000 --> 00:05:58,000
the Scout Master announced

146
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:00,000
that the scouts had selected me.

147
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:02,000
I don't think I've ever felt so humbled

148
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:04,000
as I did last night to receive that honor.

149
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:06,000
Wow, that's great news, Chris.

150
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:08,000
And that's a really nice tradition

151
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:10,000
for the scouts to do that annually.

152
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:12,000
And congratulations.

153
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:14,000
It sounds like you're having a wonderful time

154
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:16,000
and scouting with your boys.

155
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:18,000
Just so happy to hear it.

156
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:21,000
As it is Memorial Day weekend,

157
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:23,000
I'm going to go ahead

158
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:25,000
and re-broadcast a couple of favorites

159
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:27,000
from the archives.

160
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:29,000
One of them is talking about

161
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:32,000
volunteering with some boundaries

162
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:34,000
and that'll be in Scout Mastership

163
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:36,000
in seven minutes or less.

164
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:38,000
And then I've got a story to tell you

165
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:40,000
from Scout Camp

166
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:42,000
about the night the helicopter landed in camp.

167
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:45,000
Yeah, one of my favorite stories

168
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:47,000
to tell from camp.

169
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:49,000
So that sounds like enough for this podcast.

170
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:51,000
So let's get started, shall we?

171
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:54,000
Scout Mastership in seven minutes

172
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:56,000
or less.

173
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:04,000
Have you ever heard somebody call

174
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:07,000
being described as being generous to a fault?

175
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:11,000
I guess

176
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:14,000
ultimately,

177
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:16,000
you could give away everything that you own.

178
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:20,000
But then you wouldn't really have the resources

179
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:22,000
to perpetuate being generous, would you?

180
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:26,000
So generosity

181
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:28,000
is a little bit of a paradoxical value.

182
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:31,000
We can't continue being generous

183
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:34,000
unless we have

184
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:36,000
maintained some store

185
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:38,000
of resources

186
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:40,000
to share.

187
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:42,000
Volunteering

188
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:45,000
and I guess we're, you know,

189
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:47,000
absolutely talking in the specific

190
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:49,000
area of scouting.

191
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:51,000
But volunteering

192
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:53,000
is a form of generosity

193
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:55,000
that if you push it too far

194
00:07:56,000 --> 00:07:57,000
is going to bankrupt the virtue

195
00:07:58,000 --> 00:07:59,000
that creates it.

196
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:01,000
Scouting

197
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:03,000
and church and school

198
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:05,000
and charities and any other form of volunteer

199
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:07,000
where it can demand

200
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:09,000
ever greater commitments of time

201
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:11,000
and resources until the poor volunteer

202
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:13,000
just reaches a breaking point

203
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:14,000
you can call it burnout

204
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:16,000
or fatigue or what have you.

205
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:18,000
This happens when the volunteer reaches

206
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:20,000
that point of frustration

207
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:22,000
that just, you know,

208
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:24,000
they can't stand it anymore.

209
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:26,000
You've seen it happen.

210
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:28,000
If you've been around scouting long enough

211
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:30,000
you've probably seen it happen.

212
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:32,000
Maybe it's getting ready to happen to you.

213
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:34,000
So being able to continue on as a volunteer

214
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:36,000
as a scouter

215
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:38,000
means you really have to have some boundaries.

216
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:41,000
And if we don't set boundaries

217
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:43,000
there's a good chance we won't be volunteering

218
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:45,000
for very long.

219
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:47,000
We have responsibilities

220
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:49,000
of family and career

221
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:51,000
and the work that we do volunteering

222
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:53,000
and we have to continuously

223
00:08:54,000 --> 00:08:55,000
measure and prioritize these things

224
00:08:56,000 --> 00:08:57,000
and they can go way off balance.

225
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:01,000
Here's some thoughts

226
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:04,000
that can lead

227
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:06,000
a volunteer to go way off balance

228
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:09,000
and to, you know,

229
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:12,000
not have a proper set of boundaries.

230
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:15,000
The first thought would be

231
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:17,000
if I don't do it

232
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:19,000
nobody else will.

233
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:22,000
Well,

234
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:24,000
I got to tell you honestly

235
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:26,000
more than likely

236
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:28,000
if you think

237
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:30,000
that you won't, you don't do it

238
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:31,000
nobody else will

239
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:33,000
it's more or less you're saying

240
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:35,000
if I don't do it nobody else

241
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:36,000
will do it the way I think it should be done.

242
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:38,000
Am I right?

243
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:40,000
I mean there are tasks

244
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:42,000
and positions of extraordinary importance

245
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:44,000
but they're fewer and farther

246
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:46,000
between than we think.

247
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:48,000
If nobody else will take on

248
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:50,000
a given position or a task

249
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:52,000
it might be because it's really

250
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:54,000
not as important as we once thought

251
00:09:55,000 --> 00:09:56,000
or

252
00:09:57,000 --> 00:09:58,000
that position has been

253
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:00,000
grossly overinflated

254
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:02,000
by a previous volunteer

255
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:04,000
who kind of ignored their own boundaries.

256
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:08,000
Another thought

257
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:10,000
experience

258
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:12,000
is nobody really knows how to do this

259
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:14,000
like I do.

260
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:17,000
Yeah, that might be true

261
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:19,000
so train somebody else how to do it

262
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:21,000
now truthfully nobody's going to do it

263
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:23,000
just like you do

264
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:26,000
but things will change

265
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:29,000
and change is not always bad

266
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:31,000
you heard it here first

267
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:33,000
change is not always bad

268
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:35,000
how about this

269
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:37,000
how about this thought

270
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:38,000
how about this myself

271
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:40,000
rather than to show somebody else how it's done

272
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:42,000
how many times have you thought that

273
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:46,000
it could be true

274
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:48,000
but volunteering is not always about efficiency

275
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:50,000
scouting is not always about efficiency

276
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:52,000
sometimes it's about

277
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:54,000
opening up opportunities

278
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:56,000
to let other people enjoy

279
00:10:57,000 --> 00:10:58,000
the accomplishment

280
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:00,000
of volunteering

281
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:02,000
how about this thought

282
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:04,000
nobody really knows how to do this

283
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:06,000
like I do

284
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:08,000
they're not going to do it

285
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:10,000
and they're not going to do it exactly like you do

286
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:12,000
and that's fine

287
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:14,000
because they need to have the freedom

288
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:16,000
and the latitude that you've had

289
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:18,000
in developing your particular style

290
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:20,000
of working on something

291
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:22,000
don't they

292
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:24,000
isn't that the way we remain satisfied

293
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:26,000
in this kind of work

294
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:28,000
things are going to change

295
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:30,000
and change isn't always bad

296
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:33,000
staying viable

297
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:35,000
and being a contributing member

298
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:36,000
of the volunteer team is preferable

299
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:38,000
to like flaming out

300
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:40,000
in a short blaze of glory

301
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:42,000
things begin to spin out of control

302
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:44,000
if we ignore

303
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:46,000
these factors

304
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:48,000
and the first factor

305
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:50,000
is that emotional investments

306
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:52,000
and what you're doing as a volunteer

307
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:54,000
can skew your judgment

308
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:56,000
here's a really good example

309
00:11:57,000 --> 00:11:58,000
a parent who volunteers to help with an organization

310
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:00,000
that serves their child

311
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:02,000
can't ever really stop being a parent

312
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:04,000
they can try

313
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:06,000
but it's almost impossible

314
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:08,000
it's hard to be an unbiased volunteer

315
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:10,000
when your child's involved

316
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:12,000
long time volunteers

317
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:14,000
who've put a lot of blood sweat

318
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:16,000
and tears into something

319
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:18,000
tend to be overly defensive

320
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:20,000
in their work

321
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:22,000
that's another kind of emotional investment

322
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:24,000
this is my deal

323
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:26,000
and I don't want anybody else

324
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:28,000
screwing it up

325
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:30,000
these two common emotional issues

326
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:32,000
can be huge blind spots

327
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:34,000
to progress

328
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:37,000
to unemotionally evaluate situations

329
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:39,000
in our volunteer lives

330
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:43,000
sometimes

331
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:45,000
we just can't leave

332
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:47,000
delegated responsibilities alone

333
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:49,000
do you know what I'm talking about

334
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:51,000
you give somebody a job

335
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:53,000
and then you want to micromanage it

336
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:55,000
because you want it done just so

337
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:57,000
well

338
00:12:58,000 --> 00:12:59,000
we have to allow other people

339
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:01,000
some autonomy in their work

340
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:03,000
good management and leadership principles

341
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:05,000
but the incentives are different

342
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:07,000
than the work we do for pay

343
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:10,000
and some people don't respond

344
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:12,000
to the incentives of volunteering

345
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:14,000
with the same energy and integrity

346
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:16,000
as they do when a check is involved

347
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:20,000
people will break commitments

348
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:22,000
or make minimal efforts

349
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:24,000
that they wouldn't consider appropriate

350
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:26,000
in their professional life

351
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:28,000
and we really can't change that

352
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:30,000
we can only factor it into the equation

353
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:32,000
like ways

354
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:35,000
that some people are not really great volunteers

355
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:37,000
as an excuse not to delegate

356
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:39,000
you got to delegate things off

357
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:41,000
and let people do what they're going to do

358
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:44,000
we need to focus on the goal

359
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:47,000
and all of our immediate goals

360
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:49,000
need to serve the ultimate goal

361
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:51,000
maintaining a vision

362
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:53,000
what we're trying to accomplish

363
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:55,000
is as important as the work itself

364
00:13:56,000 --> 00:13:57,000
if we'll save a lot of wasted effort

365
00:13:58,000 --> 00:13:59,000
and heartache

366
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:01,000
if we concentrate on the goals of our work

367
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:02,000
we're tied up in the methods

368
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:04,000
and trying to figure that out

369
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:06,000
let's just keep an eye on the goal

370
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:08,000
and if it's scouting

371
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:10,000
the goal is the success of your scouts

372
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:12,000
we have to know our limits

373
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:14,000
I don't know my limits

374
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:16,000
you don't know your limits

375
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:18,000
few people

376
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:20,000
really see themselves that clearly

377
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:22,000
now who knows your limits

378
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:24,000
well your spouse knows your limits

379
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:26,000
and you've got a few good friends

380
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:28,000
that know your limits

381
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:30,000
and we need to ask them

382
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:32,000
are you doing too much

383
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:34,000
do you think that I'm overextended

384
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:37,000
and then we need to listen to the reply

385
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:41,000
sometimes the easiest way

386
00:14:42,000 --> 00:14:43,000
to fix your life as a volunteer

387
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:45,000
is a little two letter word

388
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:47,000
and oh no

389
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:49,000
sometimes it's the right answer

390
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:51,000
we

391
00:14:52,000 --> 00:14:53,000
are fortunate to have

392
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:55,000
these wonderful people

393
00:14:56,000 --> 00:14:57,000
who dedicate their time to scouting

394
00:14:58,000 --> 00:14:59,000
and they have a sense of responsibility

395
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:01,000
but sometimes that sense of responsibility

396
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:03,000
it's easy to drive

397
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:05,000
them to greater responsibilities

398
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:07,000
with a little bit of guilt

399
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:09,000
you know we're really having a problem with this

400
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:11,000
nobody else will do it

401
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:13,000
I mean we're nice people

402
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:16,000
but we can't allow ourselves

403
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:18,000
to be compelled by a sense of duty

404
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:20,000
or guilt alone

405
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:22,000
we have to have a sense of proportion

406
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:24,000
and responsibility

407
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:26,000
for our own lives

408
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:28,000
and sometimes that sense

409
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:31,000
volunteering is a tremendously gratifying

410
00:15:32,000 --> 00:15:33,000
and enriching experience

411
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:35,000
and we can't allow

412
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:38,000
ourselves to be pushed beyond reasonable boundaries

413
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:40,000
if we're to stay volunteers

414
00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:42,000
and we're to be effective

415
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:44,000
in the work that we did

416
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:46,000
this has to be the truth folks

417
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:48,000
because there is no

418
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:50,000
way

419
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:52,000
anyone could make this up

420
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:57,000
some of the best times I ever spent

421
00:15:57,000 --> 00:15:58,000
in scouting was

422
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:01,000
the years that I worked

423
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:03,000
as a staff member

424
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:05,000
at our summer camp

425
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:07,000
it was just a wonderful time

426
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:09,000
and we got to see some things

427
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:11,000
that you know a general person

428
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:13,000
going down and camping

429
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:15,000
and kind of hanging out

430
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:17,000
would not be able to see

431
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:19,000
some of these were good

432
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:21,000
some of these were bad

433
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:23,000
but one night stands out

434
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:25,000
as being a really remarkable

435
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:26,000
person

436
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:28,000
at our camp

437
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:32,000
so around about

438
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:34,000
I guess around about ten o'clock

439
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:36,000
tap sounds and the campers

440
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:38,000
are all off to their campsites

441
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:40,000
and off to bed

442
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:42,000
and one of my friends

443
00:16:43,000 --> 00:16:44,000
and I decided we were going to head

444
00:16:45,000 --> 00:16:46,000
into town

445
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:48,000
and so we took the old camp road

446
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:50,000
out and you know the camp road

447
00:16:51,000 --> 00:16:52,000
is about a mile long or so

448
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:54,000
it's you know dirt and gravel

449
00:16:55,000 --> 00:16:56,000
and that's kind of unusual

450
00:16:57,000 --> 00:16:58,000
at this time of night

451
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:00,000
for somebody to be headed into camp

452
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:02,000
and they're kind of swerving around

453
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:04,000
and things like that

454
00:17:05,000 --> 00:17:06,000
and we see this car approaching

455
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:08,000
so we kind of stop

456
00:17:09,000 --> 00:17:10,000
and we signal them to stop

457
00:17:11,000 --> 00:17:12,000
and there's a couple of fellas

458
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:14,000
in the car who we don't know

459
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:16,000
and we ask them if they're

460
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:18,000
camped there this week

461
00:17:19,000 --> 00:17:20,000
and they say no we're kind of lost

462
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:22,000
and we say well you know

463
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:24,000
you're headed into a scout camp

464
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:27,000
and it appeared to us

465
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:29,000
as though these guys had

466
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:31,000
had a few too many

467
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:33,000
and we're very confused

468
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:35,000
about where they were headed

469
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:43,000
so they said yeah well you know

470
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:45,000
we'll turn around I guess

471
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:47,000
we'll head on out

472
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:49,000
and they were kind of arguing

473
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:51,000
with each other

474
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:53,000
but I got back in our car

475
00:17:53,000 --> 00:17:54,000
and we proceeded to

476
00:17:55,000 --> 00:17:56,000
head out the road

477
00:17:57,000 --> 00:17:58,000
imagining these guys would turn around

478
00:17:59,000 --> 00:18:00,000
and head out with us

479
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:02,000
and nope

480
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:04,000
they just kept on going down into camp

481
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:06,000
when we saw that

482
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:08,000
and so we turned around

483
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:10,000
and we followed them into camp

484
00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:12,000
and we stopped them

485
00:18:13,000 --> 00:18:14,000
and oh one thing led to another

486
00:18:15,000 --> 00:18:16,000
and you know it turned out to be

487
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:18,000
a couple of guys who had a bit

488
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:20,000
too much to drink

489
00:18:21,000 --> 00:18:22,000
and we called the police

490
00:18:23,000 --> 00:18:24,000
to go and

491
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:27,000
wait at the gate

492
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:29,000
for the police to show up

493
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:31,000
so we were waiting there

494
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:33,000
for 10 or 15 minutes

495
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:35,000
and we see some emergency lights

496
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:37,000
up on the main road

497
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:39,000
and we say well they're the police

498
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:41,000
they're coming in

499
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:43,000
and so we're sitting at the gate

500
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:45,000
and I stepped out into the road

501
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:47,000
and kind of waved to indicate

502
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:49,000
that we were there

503
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:51,000
we were going to lead them into camp

504
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:53,000
comes through

505
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:55,000
screaming

506
00:18:55,000 --> 00:18:56,000
through the gate of the camp

507
00:18:57,000 --> 00:18:58,000
right past us

508
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:00,000
beeping the horns, sirens and everything

509
00:19:01,000 --> 00:19:02,000
and starts screaming down into camp

510
00:19:03,000 --> 00:19:04,000
a fire truck

511
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:11,000
so I looked at my buddy

512
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:13,000
and he looked at me

513
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:15,000
and we said we better get back down into camp

514
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:17,000
something's wrong

515
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:19,000
so we got in the car

516
00:19:19,000 --> 00:19:20,000
and we went down

517
00:19:21,000 --> 00:19:22,000
following this fire truck

518
00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:24,000
and we get a certain way down the road

519
00:19:25,000 --> 00:19:26,000
and all of a sudden

520
00:19:27,000 --> 00:19:28,000
there is this amazing

521
00:19:29,000 --> 00:19:30,000
bright light

522
00:19:31,000 --> 00:19:32,000
shining down from the sky

523
00:19:33,000 --> 00:19:34,000
into a grove of trees

524
00:19:35,000 --> 00:19:36,000
beside the road

525
00:19:37,000 --> 00:19:38,000
and we were really freaked out

526
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:40,000
because it looked

527
00:19:41,000 --> 00:19:42,000
like an alien invasion

528
00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:44,000
I'm not kidding

529
00:19:45,000 --> 00:19:46,000
I mean it was this tremendously

530
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:48,000
bright spotlight

531
00:19:49,000 --> 00:19:51,000
and we hit the gas

532
00:19:52,000 --> 00:19:53,000
he said oh my gosh

533
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:55,000
what in the world is happening

534
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:02,000
and we started heading back down the road

535
00:20:03,000 --> 00:20:04,000
into camp

536
00:20:05,000 --> 00:20:06,000
and we got into camp

537
00:20:07,000 --> 00:20:08,000
and we found out

538
00:20:09,000 --> 00:20:10,000
that what had happened

539
00:20:11,000 --> 00:20:12,000
is there had been a staff swim

540
00:20:13,000 --> 00:20:14,000
at the pool after taps

541
00:20:15,000 --> 00:20:16,000
and one of the fellas

542
00:20:17,000 --> 00:20:18,000
had injured himself

543
00:20:19,000 --> 00:20:20,000
and they got so upset about the injury

544
00:20:21,000 --> 00:20:22,000
they decided they would call in

545
00:20:23,000 --> 00:20:24,000
the Medevac unit

546
00:20:25,000 --> 00:20:26,000
and this is what the bright light was

547
00:20:27,000 --> 00:20:28,000
it was this helicopter

548
00:20:29,000 --> 00:20:30,000
with I guess they call it a midnight sun

549
00:20:31,000 --> 00:20:32,000
and it's properly named

550
00:20:33,000 --> 00:20:34,000
it's this giant search light

551
00:20:35,000 --> 00:20:36,000
on the helicopter

552
00:20:37,000 --> 00:20:38,000
of course they had no idea what the pool was

553
00:20:39,000 --> 00:20:40,000
so they were kind of sweeping through camp

554
00:20:41,000 --> 00:20:42,000
and of course they hit a lot of campsites

555
00:20:43,000 --> 00:20:44,000
and the story was

556
00:20:45,000 --> 00:20:46,000
I heard from several scout masters

557
00:20:47,000 --> 00:20:48,000
the next day

558
00:20:49,000 --> 00:20:50,000
they were kind of sweeping through camp

559
00:20:51,000 --> 00:20:52,000
and all the scouts got up

560
00:20:53,000 --> 00:20:54,000
and cheered and waved to the helicopter

561
00:20:58,000 --> 00:20:59,000
the reason

562
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:01,000
the fire truck was there

563
00:21:02,000 --> 00:21:03,000
is because apparently every time

564
00:21:04,000 --> 00:21:05,000
they call out a Medevac

565
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:07,000
they call out a fire truck in case

566
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:09,000
the helicopter crashes

567
00:21:10,000 --> 00:21:11,000
which is very reassuring isn't it

568
00:21:16,000 --> 00:21:17,000
but the Medevac

569
00:21:17,000 --> 00:21:18,000
finally set down

570
00:21:19,000 --> 00:21:20,000
and they got the guy

571
00:21:21,000 --> 00:21:22,000
and they took him out

572
00:21:23,000 --> 00:21:24,000
fortunately the injury wasn't as serious

573
00:21:25,000 --> 00:21:26,000
as anybody thought

574
00:21:27,000 --> 00:21:28,000
and he was back in camp within 24 hours

575
00:21:29,000 --> 00:21:30,000
but wow

576
00:21:31,000 --> 00:21:32,000
I'll tell you what a night

577
00:21:39,000 --> 00:21:40,000
if you ever get a chance

578
00:21:41,000 --> 00:21:42,000
to serve on camp staff

579
